<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h2>
    <p style="text-align: center;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Entrepreneurial Spirit</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> How to Market a Business Idea</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Simplify explanations of common phenomena. Communication: Resolve misunderstandings in group projects.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify explanations of business concepts for classmates using hand gestures or examples (e.g., "Marketing is like making a movie trailer; it's designed to grab people's attention while showing a sample video ad.")
            Can resolve misunderstandings in group discussions on business plans by rephrasing ideas and using simple visual examples (e.g., points to survey questions as an example, and says, "Market research means finding out what people want to buy.")
            Can clarify tasks in group projects on entrepreneurship by using a checklist to make sure everyone understands their part (e.g., "We need to describe our product and explain why people will want it.")
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images or short video clips of complex business concepts (e.g., "supply chain optimization," "market segmentation," "branding strategy"). Students work in pairs to discuss: "How would you explain this concept simply to someone who knows nothing about business?" They share their initial simplified explanations, focusing on using basic words and analogies. This immediately activates prior knowledge and the core skill of mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to simplify a business concept using an example like "Marketing is like making a movie trailer; it's designed to grab people's attention while showing a sample video ad." The teacher demonstrates using hand gestures, rephrasing, and clear, concise language. The teacher also models the pronunciation of words like "launch" /lɔːntʃ/ and "cost" /kɔːst/, highlighting the /ɔː/ sound, and "successful" /səkˈses.fəl/ demonstrating reduced vowels.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the concept of "simplifying explanations" by asking:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I use big, complex words, am I simplifying?" (Expected answer: "No, you're making it harder.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What can I use to help someone understand a difficult idea?" (Expected answer: "Examples, gestures, rephrasing, simpler words.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short video clip (2-3 minutes) of a local entrepreneur briefly explaining a new "innovation" or a "marketing strategy" for their "business," using some technical jargon. Alternatively, a transcript of such an interview can be used. The material has a professional, informative register, typical of a business interview.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding and interpreting complex information, then re-expressing it simply and clearly for a specific audience.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students watch/read the entrepreneur's explanation. First, they answer a gist question: "What is the entrepreneur's main business idea?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In small groups, students identify 2-3 complex terms or phrases used by the entrepreneur (e.g., "customer engagement metrics," "streamlining operations," "scaling the product line"). They discuss what they *think* these terms mean.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then provides a simplified version of the entrepreneur's explanation or a brief definition of the jargon. Students compare their initial interpretations with the simplified explanations, noting how the language was mediated. The teacher emphasizes using phrases like, "What strategies have increased customer engagement?" and "If we use social media, we can reach a larger audience."</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Simplifying explanations, rephrasing, clarifying misunderstandings, and practicing target grammar (First conditional, Present perfect, Future continuous) and vocabulary (innovation, marketing, customer base, strategy, promote, develop, enhance, implement, efficiency).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Matching Simplified Explanations:</b> Students receive a worksheet with a list of business terms (e.g., "automation," "target audience," "return on investment," "promotional events") and two columns of explanations: one complex and one simplified. They must match each term to its simplified explanation. Example: "Marketing research means finding out what people want to buy."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - Rephrasing Sentences:</b> Students are given complex sentences related to business ideas or innovations, incorporating target grammar (e.g., "The enterprise has successfully implemented an advanced, customer-focused digital marketing strategy to enhance its online visibility and increase its customer base."). In pairs, they rewrite these sentences using simpler words and first conditional or present perfect structures (e.g., "Our business has used new digital marketing plans to show up more online and get more customers." or "If we advertise on social media, we might reach more customers."). They also integrate future continuous: "I'll be analyzing the market trends."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Explain Your Project (Simplified):</b> Students get into their "Entrepreneurial Interview Series" project groups. Each student takes turns explaining a specific part of their project (e.g., "our mission," "how we will promote the interviews," "what innovation we expect from local businesses") to a partner who pretends to be a younger sibling or a non-expert. The goal is to use clear, simple language, rephrase if the partner looks confused, and use examples or gestures. The partner provides feedback on clarity and effectiveness of mediation. Focus on respectful language: "Your idea adds an interesting approach."</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align="justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Presentation and Q&A session focused on mediating their 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in their "Entrepreneurial Interview Series" project groups. Their task is to prepare a 3-4 minute presentation where they explain their project to a hypothetical audience of community members or younger students who might not understand complex business language.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The presentation must include:
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A simplified overview of the project's *mission* and *objective*.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How they plan to *implement* the interviews and what *innovation* or *strategy* they hope to uncover from local *entrepreneurs*. (e.g., "Local shops have begun using social media to tell more people about their products.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A clear explanation of each team member's role using a simple checklist to clarify tasks (e.g., "Sarah will be doing the research, I'll be arranging the interviews, and Juan will be analyzing the market trends for the report.").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Preparation for a Q&A where they anticipate and resolve potential misunderstandings about the project, rephrasing ideas and using simple visual examples if needed (e.g., pointing to a sample interview question).</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups present to the class. The teacher and other students act as the hypothetical audience, asking clarifying questions. Students should use professional language for business ideas (e.g., "This strategy could attract more customers to our project.")</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment of learning)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align="justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and a simple Peer Assessment Rubric for the group presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align="justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups perform their simplified project overview from Stage 4. Peers use a simple rubric to evaluate the clarity and effectiveness of mediation. The teacher uses an observation checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align="justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Simplified Explanations:</b> How well were complex business concepts related to the project simplified for a general audience?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Effective Rephrasing and Examples:</b> Did students rephrase ideas when misunderstandings arose and use relevant examples or gestures?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Clarification:</b> Was the division of tasks within the group clearly explained and understood?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Integration of vocabulary (e.g., "innovation," "strategy," "customer base," "promote") and grammar (First conditional: "If we advertise...", Present perfect: "We have increased...", Future continuous: "I'll be analyzing...").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation, particularly of /ɔː/ sounds (cost, talk, launch) and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words (renewable, successful).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative Interaction:</b> Effective teamwork in resolving misunderstandings during the Q&A.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align="justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete individually:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one business concept related to our 'Entrepreneurial Interview Series' project that I successfully simplified today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did I help a group member or classmate understand a task or idea better?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Review of the lesson and connection to future learning)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align="justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> In their notebooks, students reflect on:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of simplifying business ideas or clarifying tasks for others, and how did I overcome it?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How will practicing mediation skills help me in future academic projects or in real-world situations related to entrepreneurship?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align="justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in facilitating students' mediation skills, especially in simplifying business concepts and resolving misunderstandings during the project presentation? The focused practice on rephrasing and using examples seemed effective.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students to further enhance their mediation abilities? Perhaps more specific scenarios for resolving conflict or misunderstanding within group dynamics, or dedicated practice on anticipating different levels of audience understanding.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align="justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "How to Market a Business Idea." The next theme will build upon students' collaborative and communicative skills by exploring a new scenario, potentially focusing on ethical considerations in business or global entrepreneurship, continuing to apply the 21st-century skills learned.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>